(version 1)

# Fine-pitch packages cannot meet netclass clearances between their OWN adjacent
# pads - that spacing is fixed by the package, not by the layout. U3 is a QFN-40
# on 0.40mm pitch, so pad-to-pad gaps are around 0.15mm and the exposed pad sits
# closer still, while the Power netclass asks for 0.30mm.
#
# This relaxes clearance only BETWEEN PADS OF THE SAME FOOTPRINT. Clearance from
# U3's pads to anything else on the board keeps the full netclass value, so no
# board-level rule is weakened.
#
# Note on syntax: "A.Fp_Reference" silently matches nothing in KiCad 10 - it was
# tried first and left all 78 violations in place. "A.memberOfFootprint()" is the
# working accessor.
(rule "intra-footprint pad clearance on fine-pitch parts"
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('U3') && B.memberOfFootprint('U3')")
	(constraint clearance (min 0.1mm)))

# A track leaving a U3 pad runs past the next pad at 0.40mm pitch minus the
# half-widths of both: 0.20mm for a 0.20mm track, 0.225mm for a 0.15mm neck.
# The Power class asks 0.30mm of anything on a supply or GND item, so a signal
# track next to a supply pad, or a supply escape next to a signal pad, fails by
# the package's geometry whatever the layout does. Under this rule the
# class clearance (0.3mm Power, 0.2mm signal) no longer applies between a track
# and a pad of U3: the track-to-pad distance is held at 0.2mm, the signal
# class's own clearance, so no track is allowed nearer to a U3 pad than a signal
# track already was (rulings AG and AI stand). Another rule can still allow
# less: a coupled USB3 pair's track against its partner's pad (the 0.17mm rule
# below) is lower, which is moot at U3's 0.4mm pitch. The rule names U3 and
# tracks only. Vias, zones and every other footprint, U8 and U9 included, keep
# the class clearance.
(rule "track to U3 pad on fine-pitch parts"
	(condition "A.Type == 'Track' && B.Type == 'Pad' && B.memberOfFootprint('U3')")
	(constraint clearance (min 0.2mm)))

# The two traces of a controlled-impedance pair sit closer together than the
# class clearance. USB3 and MDI keep a 0.2mm class clearance, because it also
# sets the spacing between pairs and from other nets; only the gap INSIDE one
# coupled pair gets its own, smaller number. Geometry and sources:
# hardware/fab/stackup-note.md.
#
# A.isCoupledDiffPair() is true only for the two nets of one pair that KiCad
# recognises by name (..._P with ..._N, the suffix last: the SuperSpeed TX pair
# after the AC-coupling capacitors is therefore USB3_TX_CONN_P / _CONN_N, not
# USB3_TX_P_CONN, which KiCad does not pair), so a trace of the pair against any
# other net, or another pair, still gets the class clearance. Two pads are left
# out: the spacing of the pads of one footprint is the package's, and the rule
# above for U3 already covers it. MDI needs no rule: its pair gap (0.21mm) is
# above the class clearance.
(rule "intra-pair clearance USB3"
	(condition "A.NetClass == 'USB3' && B.NetClass == 'USB3' && A.isCoupledDiffPair() && (A.Type != 'Pad' || B.Type != 'Pad')")
	(constraint clearance (min 0.17mm)))
